 "verdict": "INCREMENTAL, ROBUST, NOT A BREAKTHROUGH (survives severe-test; mechanisms are prior art). On real LoCoMo QA outcomes, paired and with a genuinely BLENDED poison (alpha=0.7, so 'credit top-1' is NOT a hidden oracle), per-item ISOLATION beats set-level outcome credit on all arms and is robust across alpha in {.5,.7,.85} x K in {4,8,16}: poison blocked 37% vs 13%, latency 411 vs 537, innocent demotion 0.0% vs 2.0% (0.000 in every cell, 10/10 convs). MECHANISM (measured directly): set-level lets the poison bank good credit from co-occurrence on correct answers (good=105) that MASKS its harm; isolation strips ~83% of those masking-goods (good=17). HONEST CEILING: even with isolation the AVERAGE poison stays net-positive (good 17 > bad 3) and is NOT blocked — a broadly-on-topic poison is genuinely useful on most queries and harmful only on its target, and outcome-credit rewards usefulness. So isolation shifts a MINORITY of poisons over the blocking line (13%->37%); it does not make outcome-accountability a strong detector. CONCLUSION: outcome-accountability is a WEAK standalone detector of blended poison; the load-bearing defense must sit at the ACTION boundary (reversibility / capability-scoping / IFC), with isolation + outcome-credit as bounded-latency support. Prior art to cite (measurement is ours, not the mechanism): RobustRAG 2405.15556 (isolate-then-aggregate); memory-poisoning outcome-trust 2601.05504; MARL blame-attribution (Triantafyllou 2107.11927); data attribution (Koh&Liang, Shapley, TracIn). Scope: one corpus (LoCoMo), one embedder (nomic), synthetic blended poison.",
